1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:06,030
Welcome. This short tutorial brings
together practical examples of ideas

2
00:00:06,070 --> 00:00:11,009
and tools for creating accessible
research and educational material.

3
00:00:11,049 --> 00:00:15,899
The aim is to make research
and teaching material easier to create,

4
00:00:15,939 --> 00:00:19,151
easier to understand,
and easier to revisit.

5
00:00:19,191 --> 00:00:23,559
The presentation also
demonstrates its own approach:

6
00:00:23,599 --> 00:00:28,570
the same slides and speaker notes
can guide a live presenter or,

7
00:00:28,610 --> 00:00:31,905
as here, an AI-generated tutorial.

8
00:00:31,945 --> 00:00:35,848
The first tool is a LaTeX
formula generator.

9
00:00:35,888 --> 00:00:38,095
Instead of remembering LaTeX syntax,

10
00:00:38,135 --> 00:00:41,068
you describe the equation
in ordinary language.

11
00:00:41,108 --> 00:00:45,876
Here, the prompt at the bottom left
asks for the integral of f of x,

12
00:00:45,916 --> 00:00:49,380
where f of x is x to the
fourth power plus three.

13
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:54,307
The assistant inserts the matching
LaTeX code into the document,

14
00:00:54,347 --> 00:00:57,888
and you can choose to keep
or undo the change.

15
00:00:57,928 --> 00:01:05,055
On the right is the compiled result:
f of x equals x to the fourth plus three,

16
00:01:05,095 --> 00:01:11,068
and its integral, x to the fifth
over five, plus three x, plus C.

17
00:01:11,108 --> 00:01:17,593
This example uses OpenAI's Prism,
linked at the bottom of the slide.

18
00:01:17,633 --> 00:01:22,565
Whatever the tool, always check
that the generated equation

19
00:01:22,605 --> 00:01:26,892
and notation match
the mathematics you intended.

20
00:01:26,932 --> 00:01:33,079
Next: creating videos with Claude,
anchored in presentation slides.

21
00:01:33,119 --> 00:01:35,470
Start with your own slides.

22
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:37,954
They define the content and the sequence,

23
00:01:37,994 --> 00:01:42,780
and act as the ground truth that keeps
the video focused on the subject matter.

24
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:48,558
Writing your own narrative anchors the AI
agent and helps to avoid hallucinations.

25
00:01:48,598 --> 00:01:51,769
Speaker notes then
supply the explanations,

26
00:01:51,809 --> 00:01:55,693
for you as the presenter
and as guidance for the AI.

27
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:57,902
With the slides and notes,

28
00:01:57,942 --> 00:02:03,675
Claude can use narration and video
tools to assemble a complete tutorial,

29
00:02:03,715 --> 00:02:04,844
like this one.

30
00:02:04,884 --> 00:02:10,281
For the voice, you can choose an
open-source voice model, as in this video,

31
00:02:10,321 --> 00:02:16,120
or a service such as ElevenLabs for a more
natural voice with better intonation.

32
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,903
You could even clone your own voice.

33
00:02:18,943 --> 00:02:23,214
That is optional,
and a question worth discussing.

34
00:02:23,254 --> 00:02:26,595
You can also ask for subtitles
in different languages,

35
00:02:26,635 --> 00:02:28,951
for more inclusion and accessibility.

36
00:02:28,991 --> 00:02:33,754
On the right are example videos
made this way, on AlphaFold 2,

37
00:02:33,794 --> 00:02:38,210
diffusion, and tokenization,
available at the link below.

38
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:44,588
Before publishing, review the narration,
the visuals, and the subtitles.

39
00:02:44,628 --> 00:02:49,903
The third idea: using video
transcripts for references.

40
00:02:49,943 --> 00:02:51,759
Long recorded lectures,

41
00:02:51,799 --> 00:02:56,588
like this one on machine learning
for protein structure prediction,

42
00:02:56,628 --> 00:02:59,970
are hard to search
by scrubbing through them.

43
00:03:00,010 --> 00:03:04,071
The lecture is linked
at the top left of the slide.

44
00:03:04,111 --> 00:03:06,540
On YouTube, you can open the transcript

45
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:09,618
with the Show transcript
button below the video.

46
00:03:09,658 --> 00:03:15,754
It opens a panel where every line
of spoken text is linked to a timestamp.

47
00:03:15,794 --> 00:03:21,460
Give an AI assistant the video's URL
and ask it to read the transcript

48
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:25,657
and return the timestamp
of a specific explanation.

49
00:03:25,697 --> 00:03:28,737
You can also supply
the transcript directly.

50
00:03:28,777 --> 00:03:32,289
Automatic transcripts can contain errors,

51
00:03:32,329 --> 00:03:39,086
so watch the actual passage and check
its context before citing or sharing it.

52
00:03:39,126 --> 00:03:42,234
The fourth idea is interactive graphs.

53
00:03:42,274 --> 00:03:46,489
When many curves share one
plot, as in the lower chart,

54
00:03:46,529 --> 00:03:49,468
following any single one is difficult.

55
00:03:49,508 --> 00:03:54,150
Both charts show ROC curves
per forecast lead time,

56
00:03:54,190 --> 00:03:58,116
comparing a neural
network with a baseline.

57
00:03:58,156 --> 00:04:01,060
In the upper chart,
one scenario stands out

58
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:03,975
while the others fade into the background.

59
00:04:04,015 --> 00:04:08,402
That is because a forecast scenario
was selected in the legend,

60
00:04:08,442 --> 00:04:10,901
which makes its graph more salient.

61
00:04:10,941 --> 00:04:15,624
This is possible because Weights
and Biases supports Vega-Lite,

62
00:04:15,664 --> 00:04:19,562
a language for customizing
charts for accessibility.

63
00:04:19,602 --> 00:04:23,687
With it, you can adjust colors,
labels, and legends,

64
00:04:23,727 --> 00:04:29,511
and add interactive highlighting when
hovering over or clicking on lines.

65
00:04:29,551 --> 00:04:33,558
Choices like these make
comparisons easier to follow.

66
00:04:33,598 --> 00:04:36,888
So aim for readable colors
and clear labels,

67
00:04:36,928 --> 00:04:39,976
as well as interactive highlighting.

68
00:04:40,023 --> 00:04:44,892
The last idea: intuitive visualization
and animation libraries.

69
00:04:44,932 --> 00:04:47,116
Visualization and animation can

70
00:04:47,156 --> 00:04:51,199
make a mathematical process
easier to follow, step by step.

71
00:04:51,239 --> 00:04:55,883
At the top is smalldiffusion,
a library focused on diffusion models.

72
00:04:55,923 --> 00:05:01,248
These panels show how its output
changes as the guidance scale,

73
00:05:01,288 --> 00:05:05,036
labelled CFG scale,
goes from zero to three.

74
00:05:05,076 --> 00:05:09,219
Below is Manim, a more general
mathematical animation engine,

75
00:05:09,259 --> 00:05:13,647
shown here with a curve, a secant
line, and the area beneath it.

76
00:05:13,687 --> 00:05:16,715
Because the animation
is generated from code,

77
00:05:16,755 --> 00:05:20,047
libraries like these help
reduce the hallucinations

78
00:05:20,087 --> 00:05:23,554
that often arise from purely
video-based generators.

79
00:05:23,594 --> 00:05:27,811
You can give them to an AI
agent as dependencies,

80
00:05:27,851 --> 00:05:31,935
so it can build the visual
explanation for you.

81
00:05:31,975 --> 00:05:34,421
As always, inspect the code and check

82
00:05:34,461 --> 00:05:37,951
that the animation represents
the concept correctly.

83
00:05:37,991 --> 00:05:40,791
A good way to start is with one concept

84
00:05:40,831 --> 00:05:44,024
that would benefit
from a visual explanation.

85
00:05:44,064 --> 00:05:47,931
Then review and refine the result.

86
00:05:47,971 --> 00:05:51,976
Finally, this slide collects
the links to the tools

87
00:05:52,016 --> 00:05:55,665
and examples used
throughout the presentation.

88
00:05:55,705 --> 00:05:59,388
Prism for LaTeX editing,
the video tutorial examples,

89
00:05:59,428 --> 00:06:02,389
Weights and Biases
for experiment tracking,

90
00:06:02,429 --> 00:06:05,020
and Vega-Lite for interactive graphs.

91
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:08,641
Then smalldiffusion for diffusion
model visualizations,

92
00:06:08,681 --> 00:06:13,488
Manim for mathematical animations,
and the example video with timestamps.

93
00:06:13,528 --> 00:06:20,267
The project page gathers everything in one
place: the downloadable presentation,

94
00:06:20,307 --> 00:06:25,444
the poster, the video, voice
samples, and resource links.

95
00:06:25,484 --> 00:06:31,283
That's all for this overview.
Thank you for watching.

